e then they chase the cow.\n18.If someone likes the rabbit then they like the cow.\n19.If someone likes the cow then they see the cow.\n20.If someone sees the cow and they chase the squirrel then they see the rabbit.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the cow does not chase the squirrel.So, I need to determine if the statement "The cow does not chase the squirrel" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nLooking at statement 1: "The cow chases the squirrel."This directly states that the cow does chase the squirrel.So, if the cow is chasing the squirrel, then the statement "The cow does not chase the squirrel" would be false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I\'m not missing anything.Sometimes in logic puzzles, there can be indirect implications or maybe the statements can be used to infer other things.Let me check if there\'s any other information that could affect this conclusion.\n\nStatement 3 says the cow sees the squirrel.Statement 8 says the rabbit chases the cow.Statement 10 says the squirrel chases the rabbit.Statement 12 says the squirrel is young.Statement 14 says if someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big.But the cow likes the rabbit (statement 8), and the cow sees the squirrel (statement 3).So, the cow likes the rabbit and sees the squirrel, so statement 14 doesn\'t apply to the cow because the cow does see the squirrel.Therefore, the cow\'s size isn\'t determined by statement 14.\n\nStatement 13 says if someone chases the cow, then the cow is big.But statement 1 says the cow chases the squirrel, not the cow chasing itself.So, the cow chasing the squirrel doesn\'t directly affect whether the cow is big or not.However, if the cow is big, statement 15 says it likes the rabbit, which it does (statement 8).The cow does not chase the squirrel.</think>.\n\nThe cow chases the squirrel, as stated directly. Therefore, the option is']]
