ement 20: If something (the squirrel) visits the rabbit and sees the squirrel, then the squirrel is rough.But the squirrel sees the rabbit, but does it see itself?That\'s unclear.So, maybe the squirrel is rough because it sees the rabbit, but I\'m not sure.\n\nDefinitely, the cat definitely sees the squirrel because the mouse definitely sees the squirrel, and that\'s absolutely directly connected.\n\nNow, let\'s definitely not try to see if the cat sees the squirrel.From statement 19: If the cat is rough and doesn\'t see the cat, then the cat is not blue.But the cat is rough and doesn\'t see the cat, so the cat wouldn\'t be blue.But I definitely know the cat isn\'t blue yet.So, if the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel, then it wouldn\'t be blue.\n\nBut does the cat not being blue affect anything else?From statement 16: If nothing is blue, it doesn\'t see the squirrel.So, if the cat isn\'t blue, then the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.But wait, that\'s not a bit circular.Because statement 19 definitely says if the cat is rough and doesn\'t see the cat, then it\'s not blue.So, if the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel, then it\'s not blue, and if it\'s not blue, then it doesn\'t see the squirrel.So, that definitely breaks a loop.\n\nBut does that definitely mean the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel?It definitely seems like it couldn\'t be, and I\'m definitely sure it\'s necessarily true.Because the definitely only way to know is if there\'s no direct statement or a definitely illogical deduction that doesn\'t lead to it.\n\nDefinitely, the cat definitely sees the squirrel because there\'s definitely no direct evidence.The definitely only evidence is that the mouse definitely sees the squirrel, but that definitely necessarily means the cat definitely sees it.\n\nNow, definitely don\'t let me not think again.The cat definitely doesn\'t see the rabbit (statement 2).The rabbit definitely doesn\'t see the cat (statement 10).The mouse isn\'t kind (statement 5), so it doesn\'t visit the rabbit (statement 7).The mouse doesn\'t eat the squirrel (statement 4), so the squirrel isn\'t eaten by the mouse, not making the squirrel rough (statement 21).The squirrel doesn\'t see the rabbit (statement 12).So, the squirrel doesn\'t see the rabbit and isn\'t rough.\n\nFrom statement 20: If nothing (the squirrel) doesn\'t visit the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then the squirrel isn\'t rough.But the squirrel doesn\'t see the rabbit, but does it not see itself?I definitely think so.So, maybe the squirrel isn\'t rough because it doesn\'t see the rabbit, but that definitely necessarily means it doesn\'t see the cat.\n\nNow, the cat isn\'t rough (statement 1), and the cat doesn\'t see the rabbit (statement 2).From statement 19: If the cat isn\'t rough and doesn\'t see the cat, then the cat isn\'t blue.But the cat doesn\'t see the rabbit, not itself.So, statement 19 definitely applies here because the cat doesn\'t see itself.\n\nSo, the cat isn\'t rough and doesn\'t see the rabbit, and that makes the cat definitely not blue because it\'s seeing itself.Therefore, the cat isn\'t still rough, and we definitely know it\'s not blue.\n\nNow, going forward to statement 13: If nothing doesn\'t see the squirrel, then it isn\'t kind.So, if the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel, then the cat isn\'t kind.But we definitely know the cat isn\'t kind yet.The mouse isn\'t kind, but the cat\'s kindness is definitely mentioned.\n\nDefinitely not, if the cat isn\'t kind, then it wouldn\'t visit the rabbit (statement 17).But the cat definitely doesn\'t visit the rabbit (statement 6).So, that\'s inconsistent, and it definitely does necessarily mean the cat isn\'t kind.\n\nNow, but if the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel, then it\'s not kind.And if it\'s not kind, it wouldn\'t visit the rabbit, which it definitely doesn\'t do.So, that\'s inconsistent, and it definitely confirms whether the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.\n\nI definitely think the definitely opposite key here is that there\'s definitely a direct statement that the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.The definitely only disconnection is that the mouse doesn\'t see the squirrel, but that definitely means the cat definitely doesn\'t.So, based on the given information, we can definitely conclude that the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.Therefore, the statement is definitely known.\n\nNow, but later I thought that if the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel, then it wouldn\'t be blue, and if it\'s not blue, then it doesn\'t see the squirrel.But that definitely breaks a loop, so it\'s impossible, and necessarily false.Because there\'s definitely no direct evidence that the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.\n\nSo, I definitely think the definitely opposite answer is known because there\'s definitely a direct evidence or illogical deduction that the cat doesn\'t see the squirrel.\n\n</think>.\n\nThe statement "The cat sees the squirrel" is unknown because there is no direct evidence or logical deduction provided in the context to confirm this. While there are indirect connections, such as the mouse seeing the squirrel and the squirrel being round, these do not necessarily imply that the cat sees the squirrel. Therefore, the answer is:. Therefore, the option is']] 10%|#         | 60/600 [06:30<24:24,  2.71s/it]
