ice.But we don\'t know if she\'s cold or not.\n\nPoint 18 says all cold things are white.So if Fiona is cold, she must be white.But we don\'t know if she\'s cold.\n\nPoint 13 says all nice things are white.So if Fiona is nice, she\'s white.But from point 12, if she\'s cold, she\'s nice, which would make her white.But again, we don\'t know if she\'s cold.\n\nPoint 16 says smart, kind things are rough.So if something is smart and kind, it\'s rough.But Fiona is smart (point 8), but we don\'t know if she\'s kind.Point 20 says quiet, kind things are smart.So if a thing is quiet and kind, it\'s smart.But Fiona is smart, so maybe she\'s quiet and kind?Wait, Fiona is quiet (point 7), but we don\'t know if she\'s kind.Point 3 says Charlie is kind, but that\'s Charlie, not Fiona.\n\nPoint 19 says if Bob is kind, then Bob is white.Bob is kind (point 1), so Bob is white.But that doesn\'t affect Fiona.\n\nPoint 17 says smart, kind things are rough.So if something is smart and kind, it\'s rough.But Fiona is smart, but we don\'t know if she\'s kind.If she were kind, then she\'d be rough.But we don\'t know if she\'s kind.\n\nPoint 16 again: smart, kind things are rough.Fiona is not rough, smart, kind, or white. She may be quiet, but there\'s not enough information to determine her qualities definitively.</think>.\n\nThe statement "Fiona is rough" cannot be definitively determined as true or false based on the provided information. There\'s insufficient data to conclude that Fiona is rough. Therefore, the option is']]
