messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is cold. Anne is furry. Anne is white. Erin is cold. Fiona is round. Gary is smart. All smart things are furry. Big, blue things are round. If Gary is cold then Gary is smart. All blue, furry things are big. If Gary is furry then Gary is blue. Round things are cold. Cold, white things are furry. Round things are blue.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Gary is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown', '<think>\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Gary is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:\n\n1.Anne is big.\n2.Anne is cold.\n3.Anne is furry.\n4.Anne is white.\n5.Erin is cold.\n6.Fiona is round.\n7.Gary is smart.\n8.All smart things are furry.\n9.Big, blue things are round.\n10.If Gary is cold, then Gary is smart.\n11.Round things are cold.\n12.Cold, white things are furry.\n13.Round things are blue.\n\nNow, the question is about Gary being big.So I need to see if we can conclude that Gary is big based on these statements.\n\nLet\'s break it down:\n\n- From statement 7, Gary is smart.\n- Statement 8 says all smart things are furry, so Gary is furry.\n- Statement 10 says if Gary is cold, then Gary is smart.But we already know Gary is smart, so this doesn\'t directly tell us if Gary is cold or not.\n- Statement 11 says round things are cold.So anything that\'s round must be cold.\n- Statement 13 says round things are blue.So if something is round, it\'s both cold and blue.\n- Statement 9 says big, blue things are round.So if something is big and blue, it\'s round.\n- Statement 12 says cold, white things are furry.Since Gary is smart and thus furry (from statement 8), but we don\'t know if he\'s cold or white.\n\nWait, let\'s see if we can link these together.We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry (statement 8).Now, statement 12 says cold, white things are furry.Since Gary is furry, does that mean he\'s cold and white?Not necessarily.It\'s possible that Gary is furry for other reasons, but the statement only tells us that if something is cold and white, then it\'s furry.It doesn\'t say that all furry things are cold and white.\n\nSo, Gary is furry, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold or white.However, statement 11 says round things are cold.So if something is round, it\'s cold.Statement 9 says big, blue things are round.So if something is big and blue, it\'s round, which makes it cold.\n\nBut we don\'t know if Gary is blue.Statement 13 says round things are blue, but that doesn\'t mean all blue things are round.So Gary could be blue and round, making him big, but we don\'t have information about his color.\n\nWait, let\'s see.We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry (statement 8).Statement 10: If Gary is cold, then he\'s smart.But we already know he\'s smart, so this doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold.\n\nStatement 11: Round things are cold.So if Gary is round, he\'s cold.But we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nStatement 9: Big, blue things are round.So if Gary is big and blue, he\'s round, which makes him cold.But again, we don\'t know if he\'s blue.\n\nSo, can we conclude that Gary is big?Let\'s see.We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry.But to be big, he needs to be a big, blue thing to be round (from statement 9).But we don\'t know if Gary is blue.He could be blue, making him round and thus cold, but we don\'t have information about his color.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way.Since Gary is smart, he\'s furry.From statement 12, cold, white things are furry.So if Gary is cold and white, he\'s furry.But we don\'t know if he\'s white.Anne is white, but that\'s a different person.\n\nWait, statement 12 says cold, white things are furry.So if Gary is cold and white, he\'s furry.But we already know he\'s furry because he\'s smart.So that doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold or white.\n\nI\'m getting a bit stuck here.Let me try to see if there\'s any other connection.Statement 13 says round things are blue.So if something is round, it\'s blue.But we don\'t know if Gary is round.The information provided clearly indicates that Gary is not big, blue, round, cold, furry, or smart. There is enough evidence to conclude that Gary is small, not blue, angular, warm, hairless, and unintelligent. The answer is definitely not unknown, which is option C.</think>.\n\nThe statement "Gary is big" is unknown because there\'s no direct information linking Gary\'s size to other properties like color or being round. While being big and blue would make him round, we don\'t have evidence that Gary is blue. Thus, we can\'t conclude he\'s big. Therefore, the option is']] 21%|##        | 125/600 [12:27<06:49,  1.16it/s]
