d the lion chases the cat, then it is cold.\n17.If the lion likes the cat and the cat chases the lion, then the lion is nice.\n18.If something likes the cat, then it likes the lion.\n19.If something likes the cat, then it likes the lion.(This seems redundant, same as 18)\n20.If something chases the lion, then the lion visits the bear.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the bear does not chase the lion.So, I need to determine if the bear chases the lion or not based on these statements.\n\nLooking at the given information, the bear is known to chase the cat (statement 1) and the cow (statement 2).The bear also likes the cat (statement 3) and the lion (statement 4).Additionally, the bear visits the cat (statement 5) and the cow (statement 8).The cat is red (statement 6), visits the bear (7), and the cow (8).The cow visits the lion (9), and the lion chases the cat (10).The lion is nice (11).\n\nNow, looking at the conditional statements (12-20), let\'s see if they can help us determine if the bear chases the lion.\n\nStatement 12: If something likes the bear and visits the lion, then it is green.So, if something likes the bear (which includes the bear itself, I suppose) and visits the lion, then that something is green.But we don\'t know if the bear visits the lion yet.\n\nStatement 13: If something likes the lion and the lion is nice, then it chases the lion.The bear does not chase the lion, as indicated by the statements provided, resulting in the statement "The bear does not chase the lion" being true.</think>.. Therefore, the option is']]
