hat doesn\'t necessarily mean that all rough people are white or that all cold people are white.It just links cold, white people to being rough.So, if Bob is cold, he would be white, but if he\'s not cold, he might not be white.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that all nice people are big, and all big people are red.So Bob is big and red.Now, the statement about furry, nice people being big is already covered, but I\'m not sure about his furiteness.\n\nAlso, the context mentions that all rough people are furry, and all nice, big people are rough.Since Bob is nice and big, he must be rough.Because the statement says all nice, big people are rough.So, Bob is rough.\n\nIf Bob is rough, then he must be furry because all rough people are furry.So, Bob is furry.Now, the statement also says that if someone is nice and cold, then they are white.But Bob is nice, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold.So, can Bob be cold?\n\nWait, the context also says that all cold, white people are rough.But Bob is rough, so if he were cold, he would be white.But does that mean that being rough implies he\'s cold and white?Or is it just that cold, white people are rough, but not necessarily the other way around?\n\nI think it\'s the latter.So, being rough doesn\'t necessarily mean he\'s cold or white.It just means that if someone is cold and white, they\'re rough.But Bob is rough, so he could be cold and white, but he might not be.So, we don\'t have enough information to conclude that Bob is white.\n\nWait, but let me think again.The statement says that if someone is nice and cold, then they are white.So, Bob is nice, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold.If he\'s cold, he\'s white.If he\'s not cold, he might not be white.But the context doesn\'t say that being nice and not cold would make him not white.It only says that nice and cold makes him white.\n\nSo, Bob could be white if he\'s cold, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold.Alternatively, he might not be cold, and thus not white.But the context doesn\'