hing sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is young.\n16.Cold things are big.\n17.If something chases the tiger and sees the cat, then the tiger is big.\n18.If something is big, then it chases the tiger.\n19.If something sees the bald eagle, then it sees the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger chases the tiger.Let\'s break this down.\n\nFirst, from statement 8, the tiger is big.Statement 18 says that if something is big, then it chases the tiger.So, since the tiger is big, according to statement 18, the tiger should chase the tiger.Wait, that seems a bit circular, but logically, if the tiger is big, then it chases the tiger.So that would make the statement "The tiger chases the tiger" true.\n\nBut let me check if there\'s any contradiction or other statements that might affect this conclusion.Let\'s look at statement 17: If something chases the tiger and sees the cat, then the tiger is big.Well, we already know the tiger is big, so this statement doesn\'t necessarily provide new information about whether the tiger chases itself.\nThe statement that the tiger chases the tiger is false.</think>.\n\nThe tiger is big, and according to the given statements, any big thing chases the tiger. Therefore, the option is']]
