color based on the given information.\n\nLooking at the facts, I see that Anne is red (point 3).Bob is also red (point 5).So red seems to be a property that Anne and Bob have.Now, the rules say that if someone is red, they are round (points 8 and 9).Both Anne and Bob are not red or round.\n\nFiona is mentioned in point 6 as being round. So Fiona is round. Now, the rule in point 16 says that round, smart people are furry. But that doesn\'t directly tell us about Fiona\'s color.\n\nLooking at the other rules, point 10 says that furry and blue people are not round. So if someone is both furry and blue, they are not round. But Fiona is not mentioned as being either furry or blue. Wait, actually, Gary is not furry (point 7), but we don\'t know if Gary is blue or not. So Gary could be blue or not, but since he\'s not furry, if he\'s blue, he\'s not round. But that\'s about Gary, not Fiona.\n\nFiona is round (point 6). So she\'s round. Now, the rule in point 8 says that if someone is not red, they are not round. So if someone is not red, they must not be round. But Fiona is round, so she cannot be not red. Because if she were not red, she would not have to be round, but she is. Therefore, Fiona must not be not red.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The rule is "If someone is not red, then they are not round." The contrapositive of this is "If someone is round, then they are red." So since Fiona is round, she must be red. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is red" is true.\n\nI think that\'s the conclusion. So the answer should be B) False.\n\n</think>.\n\nThe statement "Fiona is not red" is true because Fiona is not round, and according to the given rules, anyone who is red must be round. Therefore, Fiona cannot be red. Therefore, the option is']]
