and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n17.If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.\n18.If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow.\n19.If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.\n20.If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n21.If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the squirrel likes the cat.Let\'s see what we have.\n\nFrom statement 12, we know the squirrel likes the cow.So, Squirrel → Likes Cow.\n\nLooking at statement 13: If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat.So, Visits(Squirrel) → Likes Cat.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the squirrel visits the cat or not.So, we don\'t have direct information about whether the squirrel visits the cat or not.\n\nStatement 17 says that if something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.So, Likes Cat → Chases Squirrel.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the squirrel likes the cat yet, so we can\'t directly apply this.\n\nStatement 20: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.So, Chases Squirrel → Visits Cat.\n\nBut again, we don\'t know if the squirrel is chasing the squirrel or not.\n\nLet me see if there\'s any other information that can link these together.\n\nStatement 1: The cat is big.So, Cat → Big.\n\nStatement 9: Lion is not big.So, Lion → ¬Big.\n\nStatement 4: Cat visits Lion.So, Cat → Visits Lion.\n\nStatement 8: Cow does not visit Lion.So, Cow → ¬Visits Lion.\n\nStatement 6: Cow likes Lion.So, Cow → Likes Lion.\n\nStatement 10: Lion is rough.So, Lion → Rough.\n\nStatement 21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.So, Cow → Rough → Chases Cat.\n\nBut wait, statement 5 says the cow is blue, so Cow → Blue.But statement 21 says if Cow is rough, then it chases the cat.However, the cow is blue, but we don\'t know if being blue affects roughness.So, we can\'t conclude that the cow is rough just because it\'s blue.Unless there\'s a rule that being blue makes something rough, but there\'s no such statement.So, we can\'t assume that.\n\nWait, statement 10 says the lion is rough, not the cow.So, the cow being blue doesn\'t affect roughness.So, we can\'t conclude that the cow is rough, so we can\'t conclude that the cow chases the cat.So, statement 21 doesn\'t help us here.\n\nNow, going back to the squirrel.The squirrel likes the cow (statement 12).So, Squirrel → Likes Cow.\n\nWe need to find if Squirrel → Likes Cat.\n\nIs there any rule that connects liking the cow to liking the cat?Let\'s see.\n\nStatement 16: If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.So, (Likes Cow ∧ Visits Cow) → Visits Cow → Visits Cat.\n\nBut the squirrel doesn\'t necessarily visit the cow.So, unless the squirrel visits the cow, we can\'t conclude that the cow visits the cat.\n\nBut the squirrel likes the cow, but we don\'t know if it visits the cow.So, we can\'t link it directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way through other statements.\n\nLet me think about the chain of events.\n\nWe know the cat is big (1), so the cat is big.The lion is not big (9), so the lion is not big.The cat visits the lion (4).The lion is rough (10).The cat likes the cow (3).The cow is blue (5).The cow likes the lion (6).The cow does not visit the lion (7).The lion chases the cat (8).The cat is cold (2).The cat is big (1).The cat is cold (2).The cat visits the lion (4).The lion is rough (10).The lion is not big (9).The lion visits the cat (11).Wait, statement 11 says the lion visits the cat.So, Lion → Visits Cat.\n\nBut statement 19 says if something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.So, Chases Lion → Lion Chases Cow.\n\nBut the cat is chasing the lion?Wait, statement 8 says the cat chases the lion.So, Chases Lion.\n\nSo, from statement 19: Chases Lion → Lion Chases Cow.\n\nSo, since the cat is chasing the lion, the lion is chasing the cow.\n\nSo, Lion Chases Cow.\n\nBut the cow is blue (5), and the cow likes the lion (6).The cow does not visit the lion (7).So, the cow is not visiting the lion, but the lion is chasing the cow.\n\nNow, the cat is visiting the lion (4), and the cat is cold (2).So, the cat is cold.\n\nBut how does this relate to the squirrel?\n\nThe squirrel likes the cow (12).So, Squirrel → Likes Cow.\n\nThe squirrel does not like the cat.</think>.\n\nThe statement "The squirrel likes the cat" cannot be determined to be true or false based on the given information. The only facts we have are that the squirrel likes the cow, but there\'s no rule that connects the squirrel liking the cow to liking the cat. Therefore, the option is']] 56%|#####5    | 335/600 [16:51<00:50,  5.27it/s] 56%|#####6    | 336/600 [16:51<00:44,  5.89it/s]
