s any new information about whether the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit or not.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we have enough evidence to conclude whether the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit.So the statement "The bear visits the rabbit" could be false or known.\n\nGo on, but the question is asking if the statement is true, false, or known.Since we have enough information to confirm or deny that the bear doesn\'t visit the rabbit, the answer would be known.\n\nBut go on, earlier I thought that the bear doesn\'t need the tiger, and the tiger is not being visited by the bear, and that necessarily means the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit.So maybe the bear easily spots the rabbit, making the statement true.But I\'m entirely certain.\n\nPerhaps the bear easily spots the rabbit, making the statement false.But without a direct statement, it\'s easy to say.\n\nGo on, let me try to see if there\'s any other implication.From statement 19: If the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit, then the rabbit doesn\'t see the tiger.But the rabbit already doesn\'t see the tiger because the tiger doesn\'t need the rabbit.So the condition is satisfied regardless of whether the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit or not.Therefore, statement 19 doesn\'t help us determine if the bear doesn\'t see the rabbit.\n\nSo, based on the given information, we can determine whether the bear avoids the rabbit or not.Therefore, the statement "The bear visits the rabbit" is known.\n\nGo on, but the question is asking if the statement is true, false, or known.If we can determine, then the answer is known.But I\'m entirely confident because sometimes in logic puzzles, even if it\'s directly stated, you can\'t infer based on the implications.\n\nGo on, let\'s think again.The bear doesn\'t need the tiger (from statement 17), so the bear is not visited by the tiger.The tiger doesn\'t need the rabbit (statement 12), so the tiger is not visited by the rabbit.The rabbit doesn\'t need the bear (statement 10), so the rabbit 