and smart, which makes him young.But that contradicts point 4, which says Charlie is cold.Wait, no, point 4 just says Charlie is cold; it doesn\'t say he\'s not young.But according to point 14, since Charlie is cold and smart, he must be young.So Charlie is young.\n\nNow, point 9 says all young things are kind, and point 10 says all kind things are blue.So if Charlie is young, he\'s kind, and therefore blue.But point 5 says Charlie is kind, which aligns with that.Point 7 says Charlie is smart, and point 14 confirms he\'s young.\n\nNow, looking back at Bob.Bob is rough, so he\'s cold (point 12).But is Bob young?Let\'s see.The only information about Bob is that he\'s rough.There\'s no direct statement about Bob being young or not.However, point 11 says young things are cold, but it doesn\'t say that only cold things are young.So Bob being cold doesn\'t necessarily make him young.Unless there\'s another rule that connects Bob\'s roughness to his age.\n\nWait, point 13 says all young, blue things are kind.We know for sure that Bob is not young, smart, or necessarily kind. The statement "Bob is young" is incorrect and cannot be confirmed.</think>.\n\nThe statement "Bob is young" is unknown because the given information doesn\'t provide enough evidence to confirm or deny Bob\'s youth. Bob is cold due to being rough, but without knowing if he\'s smart, we can\'t conclude he\'s young. Therefore, the option is']]
