eeds the bald eagle then the bald eagle chases the rabbit.\n15.If something is big and it chases the rabbit then it sees the bear.\n16.If something sees the rabbit and it needs the tiger then the tiger is not blue.\n17.If something chases the rabbit then it sees the bald eagle.\n18.If the rabbit is not young and the rabbit does not need the bald eagle then the rabbit sees the bear.\n19.If something is red and it needs the bear then it is big.\n20.If something is red then it needs the bear.\n\nNow, I need to see if I can derive whether the bald eagle is big or not.\n\nLooking at the given statements, I see that the bald eagle is both green and red (statements 3 and 4).The bald eagle is not big because it is red and does not need the bear, which contradicts statement 20.</think>.\n\nThe bald eagle is big because it is red (statement 4), and from statement 20, being red means it needs the bear. Then, from statement 19, needing the bear makes it big. Therefore, the statement is true. Therefore, the option is']]
