ds the bald eagle.\n20.If something eats the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is nice.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the lion sees the lion.So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly leads to this conclusion.\n\nLooking at the statements, the lion is mentioned in a few places.Let\'s see:\n\n- Statement 8: The dog sees the lion.\n- Statement 10: The lion eats the bald eagle.\n- Statement 11: The lion is green.\n- Statement 12: The lion sees the dog.\n- Statement 13: Cold things are green.(Not directly relevant here.)\n- Statement 14: If something sees the lion, then it needs the dog.\n- Statement 16: If something is nice, then it sees the lion.\n- Statement 17: If something is big, then it sees the cat.\n- Statement 18: If something is green and needs the dog, then it eats the cat.\n- Statement 19: If something is green and eats the cat, then the cat needs the bald eagle.\n- Statement 20: If something eats the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is nice.\n\nThe lion does not see the lion.</think>.\n\nThe lion is nice (statement 11), and according to statement 16, if something is nice, it sees the lion. Therefore, the lion sees the lion. Therefore, the option is']]
