k, the context says Fiona is cold, furry, but does it say she\'s rough?Let me check again.\n\nWait, the context says:\n\n- Fiona is cold.\n- Fiona is furry.\n\nBut it doesn\'t explicitly say Fiona is rough.Hmm, but in the logical statements, there\'s a rule that if Fiona is rough and cold, then she\'s smart.But since the context doesn\'t mention Fiona being rough, we don\'t know if that condition is met.\n\n5.**Cold, furry things are white.**\n   - So, if something is both cold and furry, it\'s white.Checking the context, both Anne, Erin, and Fiona are cold and furry, and all of them are white.So that\'s consistent.\n\n6.**All rough, nice things are young.**\n   - So, if something is both rough and nice, it must be young.Let\'s see if any of the characters fit this.Erin is rough, nice, and young.Bob is rough, but he\'s also nice?Wait, the context says Bob is nice, rough, cold, furry, smart, young.So Bob is rough and nice, which would make him young, which he is.That\'s consistent.\n\n7.**If something is white and furry, then it is smart.**\n   - So, if something is white and furry, it must be smart.Checking the context, Anne is white, furry, and smart.Erin is white, furry, and smart.Fiona is white, furry, but does the context say she\'s smart?Fiona is definitely young based on the information provided in the context. Erin and Bob are both young, and they share similar characteristics with Fiona. Fiona being cold and furry suggests she is also young, according to the rule. Additionally, the rule indicates that all rough, nice things are young, so if Fiona is rough and nice, she must be young. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is not young" is incorrect based on the evidence presented.</think>.. Therefore, the option is']]
