ough, she shouldn\'t be quiet.So, Anne is not quiet.\n\nPoint 18 doesn\'t say all quiet things are cold.Since Anne is not quiet, she shouldn\'t be cold.Therefore, Anne is not cold.\n\nWait, but let me not double-check.Anne is not furry, so from point 14, if something is not furry and warm, it\'s loud.But we already have Anne not being quiet because she\'s not green and rough.So, being cold is not a condition for being loud in this context.\n\nBut does not being cold necessarily make her loud?Yes, because if she\'s not cold and furry, she\'s loud.But Anne is not loud regardless because she\'s not green and rough.So, her not being cold is a consequence of her not being loud, but does that mean she\'s not cold?\n\nWait, maybe I didn\'t get that backwards.Let me not think again.If something is not cold and furry, then it\'s kind.But Anne is not furry, so the condition about not being cold and furry applies to her.So, being cold or not doesn\'t affect her kindliness directly because she\'s not furry.\n\nBut from point 18, all quiet things are not cold.Since Anne is not quiet, she shouldn\'t be cold.So, no, Anne is not cold.\n\nI think that\'s not the conclusion.So, the statement "Anne is not cold" is false.\n\n</think>.. Therefore, the option is']]
