ouse without the mouse eating it.\n\nBut then, statement 10 says the squirrel visits the mouse, so that would make the statement true.However, there\'s a conflict because if the mouse sees the cow, it would eat the squirrel, but if the mouse doesn\'t see the cow, then it doesn\'t eat the squirrel.\n\nWait, maybe the fact that the cow is visiting the mouse doesn\'t necessarily mean the mouse sees the cow.Maybe visiting is a different action.So the mouse might not see the cow, so the mouse doesn\'t eat the squirrel, allowing the squirrel to visit the mouse.\n\nBut then, statement 10 says the squirrel visits the mouse, so that would be true.However, there\'s another angle: statement 12 says if the cow eats the mouse and the cow eats the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cow.But the cow doesn\'t eat the mouse (statement 4) or the squirrel (since the mouse is eating the squirrel if the mouse sees it).Wait, no, the cow doesn\'t eat the mouse because statement 4 says the lion does not eat the mouse, but it doesn\'t say the cow doesn\'t eat the mouse.Wait, statement 4 says the lion does not eat the mouse, but the cow is visiting the mouse, which might mean the cow is eating the mouse, but statement 4 only says the lion doesn\'t eat the mouse.So perhaps the cow is eating the mouse.\n\nLet\'s complicate this further. I\'ll stick to the same approach.\n\nFirst, let\'s ignore all the facts:\n\n- Cow isn\'t green, it\'s blue.\n- Cow avoids mouse.\n- Mouse is neither blue nor red.\n- Squirrel is not red and doesn\'t visit mouse.\n- If something doesn\'t eat squirrel, then squirrel avoids cow.\n- If cow doesn\'t eat mouse and cow doesn\'t eat squirrel, then squirrel avoids cow.\n- If something is not red or small, it doesn\'t eat lion.\n- If something avoids cow, it also avoids squirrel.\n- If something misses mouse, mouse avoids squirrel.\n- If something misses lion, it misses mouse.\n- If something is not red and avoids lion, it eats cow.\n- If something is red, it avoids l